#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](images/10/10c3a1128f78cd19b14ca29bda23bcd3c14a49a6bbf2608aa568f6f7c22aea9b.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](images/83/837556eba2375812c7d20c59533fc51f91716c6306254d2fc193b98315dd5663.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/20/20ee95979c2ae9a6f3eca54e65923f608ef569141c6ff1393ef53e99636dda1a.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/13/13f61ba37464f828258713594c973c4ab7c5c1d6aa50c2b482dcc2b444013686.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](images/69/69506c47b7fce7fd91eee135299db2920598325d4eb28611d758aeb5e3815489.svg)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5505" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/3e/3e68233c9221816c1bd834d2260a162ec6d4cc5e063022c8b9e545948f703589.svg)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](images/43/432aa48b4a3573c076a617dacc461cd5816c5279e79f9f14545609b0b4553f0c.svg)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/7c/7c7e458176039ee582b023ad53137c6adf5d66fdb7e17e2a62aedde6a160545a.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124343 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5506" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/95/95456befbc775262e58e48b5e32503b70e1a675d5c4b79911bfd3d526880d42e.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/9a/9ac30b66a0fb5232b248e7ccf6d5b57267168e328387f233dfdb793fbb7ea287.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/db/dbd8e786b6f8ffa50a35abc0136871f855f56fd4afff75c4ef6542dfa9b08f10.svg)\n\nFinally, ![](images/df/df31aa59467f4b7c37d2406d5ad6571a69baa6f9a7868f6bd868e45cf10c15aa.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7330" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0acea11970e0a11fd3d6df09c46335ca747b58d82e6cf5a3c1bdfe941ccae242.svg) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/00/001911806161a251ef55b16759d586e6d6747a8d34bae00c07eb89c8179cde84.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/41/413d4dbc71e9f4059fc86d85806e009d5d41dfd8a87b9ea159badcc6003602e5.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/18/187539cd8867c5b8592cf3b6f928992f7be756c25a9a641741ba98150072f307.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/a0/a09b2916af73b1d593ac237e5e895ff3d0c31d4978b66e4358dbd5f132547324.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} {:answer/id #uuid "4210454b-1b57-5202-b24c-d9ba774f094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269863} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa01c8c-fa5e-5db3-9890-98afc410a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270630} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b21af7-648d-570d-aab4-7958bcae5273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270762} {:answer/id #uuid "fb277ce2-2359-57d8-9955-89059a164bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269486} :answer-field/id #uuid "a98d67e2-0ccf-58ae-aa10-005055ae0213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8196086-ee7b-5323-b641-b0ce439cc41f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140262" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d28198c-10ed-558f-8e71-d5f2fb79b590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432155} {:answer/id #uuid "2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432156} {:answer/id #uuid "2efa584d-3db2-57e9-a646-6fda0b51e92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432157} {:answer/id #uuid "6054a99d-5ddb-5221-9dce-cc7c44c1f9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432160} {:answer/id #uuid "b4acffba-1ec8-59df-8d94-4699791de74a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2103dd17-24f4-5247-b56e-72b117146a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432156} :answer-field/id #uuid "883bc44f-5335-5507-b72b-47a96f7fe8f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17cb4954-c3d0-565d-94a6-8a00df11ece9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37806" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b039043-81b4-5fc5-8708-7fbf237a4ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382067} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdae987-e461-5f61-b5d9-45cafc99eb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382069} {:answer/id #uuid "972e4d42-5535-532c-a9ea-1b24b7a4b9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382070} {:answer/id #uuid "99def5b0-3eb9-5fe1-a95a-3aba50761daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c0942e1-d697-5f1d-ad8d-e9b6258b9eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6099f13f-2d83-545c-81d5-a7792b6fc7be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be37aea9-9ebb-51c3-9045-5fceee548409" :question/math-academy-id "q-37843" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f3e2af-1d0c-5ede-94d8-b2f1171885f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395873} {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} {:answer/id #uuid "11ba3c2a-7e4a-5f75-b416-64d7e2c0bb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395875} {:answer/id #uuid "842abd30-146b-5529-8174-62765bece360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395876} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e2d4db-4322-5304-a102-6991fb8129e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea3c10a-5a5b-5b56-ac38-0531c16322a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395874} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ff45fc-3165-5e0a-8ffa-750ac771413e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21c29510-ffed-5744-82ad-55435a6f5d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-37846" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d113fd3-c71b-5d9e-955d-e46183c3748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382074} {:answer/id #uuid "39618bc3-2b12-586f-bdd9-ef421e79b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382075} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} {:answer/id #uuid "04d7ca2c-b383-5d9e-84b4-63fd717e6dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382077} {:answer/id #uuid "708eb7f4-bd39-5814-8802-8b791bc777c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} :answer-field/id #uuid "271a716c-d9a8-5efe-8102-cf2499e7bd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f806fd6-4180-5914-82ca-9edb27806cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37848" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & a & -2 \\\\ -1 & b & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $(a+b)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st  and the $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $(a+b)^{2}=(0+1)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea81202-7482-5812-86cb-8eb7218d7d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432153} {:answer/id #uuid "78bcd352-9548-567a-a122-5893ecb2b938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432161} {:answer/id #uuid "94406db6-bcd3-5a97-9f39-575286530355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432162} {:answer/id #uuid "b8490153-50bf-57a4-8199-293216881d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432164} {:answer/id #uuid "ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432167} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa1985-d36d-5245-9e3f-b2f24a2cdea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "938c5c1e-3528-5126-9a4c-fc692c630173" :question/math-academy-id "q-40161" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-(4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-4({x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124532 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b239ff-66d7-5a15-b91a-27cbd26aafcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=2,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432154} {:answer/id #uuid "41f2343b-0ba1-541f-bfdd-50ec15ef6109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=0,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432158} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432159} {:answer/id #uuid "ba317e38-2bd5-592f-b3a3-98de785fc396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432165} {:answer/id #uuid "e835880d-6a15-5422-a117-36750218335a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b6b9cd9-57fe-539e-8f9a-c6c105d80efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432159} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7eb7acd-4f6f-5c51-b373-dbdb10113bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc662b9-768c-5035-bd5f-1385f3afe28a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83709" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & b \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & b \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} {:answer/id #uuid "259df9a5-e148-52b1-8c76-e5f3797156f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351913} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0b1af0-e162-51c8-a699-561daf399c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352507} {:answer/id #uuid "90343dd9-95c9-5f79-b6c6-c97b7b8b1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352816} {:answer/id #uuid "aeab5e76-18bb-5fe4-8750-1b5fd4ac3465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5e97c5-f73a-5dc7-aca2-86493283b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b7eafb-592d-5378-aed9-4f494837b137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7c3932-3369-5860-a2cf-645dd0b0d0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-84611" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
